ew": "South Dakota's Senate race pits incumbent Republican Mike Rounds against independent Brian Bengs, who became the de facto challenger after the Democratic nominee withdrew in August and the party declined to replace him. Rounds holds a decisive financial edge, with $2.7 million on hand and outside support, while Bengs has raised under $800,000 and spent most of it.\n\nThe state's Republican lean and Rounds' incumbency make him a strong favorite, and prediction markets price his win odds above 90 percent. Still, recent polling has been closer than expected, with a September survey showing a six-point race and Democratic-leaning polls even tighter. Bengs' anti-corruption message and unified anti-Rounds field give the race more life than a typical South Dakota contest, but he remains an underdog.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0278, "margin": -11.24}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0347, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0268, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0205, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0193, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0201, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0185, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0147, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0242, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1443, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0233, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.0766, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.0984, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.0493, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.0949, "margin": -7.5}, {"date": "2026-10-07", "p_dem": 0.1165, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-10-08", "p_dem": 0.0659, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-09", "p_dem": 0.0591, "margin": -9.0}]}